<h2><!--n-->. A stored connector allow-list that holds a non-string is refused by both reads with one shared message (KS-1534, item b)</h2>
<table class="pmatrix"><tr><td class="pm-topic" rowspan="3"><strong>What this changes.</strong></td><td> Kam ruled on 2026-11-05 (card <code>secuura-ks1534-allow-list-array-of-non-strings-1105</code> = a): "Refuse in both reads with one shared message (fail closed)". A new pure module, <code>services/api-gateway/src/services/connectorAllowList.ts</code>, exports the message constant and <code>allowListHoldsANonString</code>, which walks the indices so a hole counts as a non-string. <code>services/health.ts</code> (the info reader) and <code>routes/verification.ts</code> (the create path) each call it once, straight after their existing non-array guard, and answer 403 FORBIDDEN with <code>Connector document-type allow-list is not a list of strings; refusing until it is corrected</code>. The spec's <code>GET /api/connector/info</code> description and 403 text now say "not a list of strings", and the declared <code>allowedDocumentTypes: z.array(z.string())</code> is what the handler guarantees.
    </td></tr><tr><td><strong>Before:</strong> neither read checked the ELEMENTS. The info reader echoed <code>[1, 2]</code>, <code>[null]</code> or <code>['SSD_DOCUMENT', 1]</code> raw. The create path cast the array to <code>string[]</code> and ran <code>.includes</code>: <code>[1, 2]</code> refused every create as "not permitted to register document type", and a mixed list, a hole or an explicit <code>undefined</code> after a string ADMITTED an <code>SSD_DOCUMENT</code> create (measured: 201, enforcement reached). <strong>Unchanged:</strong> absent, <code>null</code> and <code>[]</code> mean no restriction on both reads; a present value that is not an array keeps the older "is not a list" refusal and text; a list of strings is echoed and enforced as before. The write side (<code>PUT /api/admin/settings</code>, KS-1230) has refused such a value since before this change.
    </td></tr><tr><td><strong>Why one module.</strong> Item (a) of this ticket pinned the two non-array guards as the same executable code (PR 1842). For the element check the predicate and the message exist once and both reads import them, so they cannot drift; the pin file's new cell G6 also refuses a literal copy of the text in either caller, which would compile and pass every behavioural cell.
    </td></tr></table>
<h3><!--n-->.1 What proves it</h3>
<table class="pmatrix"><tr><td class="pm-topic" rowspan="3"><strong>Red on develop, green on the branch, and each tamper reddens only its own cells.</strong></td><td> <strong>New file</strong> <code>ks1534-an-allow-list-of-non-strings-is-refused-by-both-reads.test.ts</code> (the ks1232 harness: the real info router and the real create route over one stored value, 11 non-string shapes): R1 info refusal, R2 create refusal for a named type, R3 for an untyped body, R4 agreement of the two reads over 18 shapes, R5 the exported constant and predicate; controls C1 (absent, null, []), C2 (non-array text unchanged), C3 (list of strings unchanged), C4 (the fixtures can tell the cases apart, including a real hole). On develop's product R1-R5 fail, each by AssertionError, and the controls pass.
    </td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tampers</strong> (applied in the worktree, restored by content, sha256 checked): both callers reverted to develop fails G6 and R1-R4; the guard disabled in health.ts only fails R1, R4, G6; in verification.ts only fails R2, R3, R4, G6; health.ts answering the older text fails R1, R4, G6; a literal copy of the same text fails G6 alone; a predicate that checks only the first element, or one that uses <code>some()</code> and so skips a hole, fails R1-R5.
    </td></tr><tr><td><strong>Whole suite:</strong> api-gateway before <strong>1654 passed, 0 failed</strong> (157 files, develop <code>7d2d6dc366e5</code>); after <strong>1666 passed, 0 failed</strong> (158 files): +12 cells, +1 file. <code>tsc --noEmit -p .</code> 0 errors; eslint clean on the six changed files (a planted <code>debugger</code> fails the same command); the test-type ratchet reads 25 errors, all in the baseline, 0 new, 158 test files examined. <code>check:openapi</code> passes and the yaml diff is two lines under <code>/api/connector/info</code>. Host: an Apple Silicon (arm64) Mac, Darwin 27.0.0, Node v26.8.1 (NOT Node 24), vitest 4.1.12. Not run: the four platform suites, a running stack, the push hook's Docker legs.
    </td></tr></table>
